    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d73">

                <pb facs="0062" n="54" sameAs="frus:frus1940v04/pg54"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.24/808: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-07-22T11:00:00+07:00">July 22, 1940—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received 2 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>356. Department’s 124, July 20, 4 p.m. I do not feel that Generalissimo expects
                    any replies to statements made to me and reported in my 338, July 12, 9 a.m. The
                    Secretary’s statements in regard to closing of Indochina and Burma
                        frontiers<anchor n="96" xml:id="d73-f1"/> reported in radio bulletins
                    numbers 168 and 169 received wide publicity here and were favorably commented
                    upon. They indicate as nothing else we might say just where we stand. I
                    therefore respectfully suggest that unless Department feels that it is very
                    important it make to the Generalissimo the statements set down in the
                    Department’s instruction under reference, the matter of the Department’s
                    suggestions to me be permitted to stand uncommented upon.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
